Optimistic parallel discrete event simulation (PDES) is widely used to accelerate large-scale simulations, but it relies on frequent state saving. For large logical process (LP) states with block-level hot/cold behavior, periodic full state saving (PSS) repeatedly copies cold data. This wastes bandwidth and memory and fails to exploit block-level write sparsity. We propose Sparse Block FULL (SBF), a block-aware sparse full-snapshot mechanism. SBF preserves full-checkpoint rollback semantics and uses OS-level write trapping to avoid redundant copies of cold blocks. We further build an online adaptive strategy that switches between SBF and PSS based on lightweight runtime cost signals. We evaluate our approach on La-pdes and Dragonfly models on ARMv8 and x86_64 clusters with up to 64 nodes under both strong and weak scaling. The results show that SBF improves committed-event throughput by up to 5.10 × over the best baseline. Dynamic switching delivers up to an additional 1.58 × speedup over the best static scheme.